 ## The probes and what each proves
 
-The probes are the sixteen hidden worksheet functions in the probes module. Each proves one thing the host can tell apart, on the way in or the way out, or one rule the bridge applies to what the host cannot. Together they are the knobs a capacity run turns: how many calls, how many cells in, how many cells out. They ship in the build everyone loads and stay as long as the seam does.
+The probes are the eighteen hidden worksheet functions in the probes module. Each proves one thing the host can tell apart, on the way in or the way out, or one rule the bridge applies to what the host cannot. Together they are the knobs a capacity run turns: how many calls, how many cells in, how many cells out. They ship in the build everyone loads and stay as long as the seam does.
 
 | Probe | In and out | Proves |
 |---|---|---|
@@ -303,6 +303,8 @@ The probes are the sixteen hidden worksheet functions in the probes module. Each
 | `CountMixed(values)` | a `MixedArray` in, a whole number out | a rectangle of three kinds arrives as one record, and its shape says how many cells |
 | `SumQuantity(header_row, trades)` | a `TextRow` and a table of a date, a text, a number and a boolean column in, a number out | a header row and a body bind by name into typed columns, one of each column type; the sum of one column proves the binding |
 | `Dot(a, b)` | two `NumberColumn`s in, a number out | two ranges in one call arrive as two ranges |
+| `FirstOfDates(dates)` | a `DateColumn` in, a text out | the date rule over a column, alone: a cell is a date when it holds a whole serial inside the calendar |
+| `FirstOfNames(names)` | a `TextColumn` in, a text out | the text rule over a column, alone, its scan for an empty text included |
 | `EchoColumn(values)` | a `NumberColumn` in and out | a column in and the same column out, spilled down |
 | `EchoRow(values)` | a `NumberRow` in and out | the same across; a row out costs what a column out costs |
 | `EchoMixed(values)` | a `MixedArray` in and out | a rectangle of three kinds goes out as it came, one tagged value per cell: the mixed road out, read over numbers alone against `EchoColumn` and over a mixed column against that |
@@ -312,7 +314,7 @@ The probes are the sixteen hidden worksheet functions in the probes module. Each
 
 `Ping` is not a probe. It is the bridge's own worksheet function, listed in the wizard, and returns `goosedove <version> from <path>`, with `source checkout` in place of the version when the package is not installed.
 
-The three looks, a column of numbers, of dates and of text, have no probe, because the host cannot tell those ranges from any other, so `FirstOfNumbers`, `FirstOfDates` and `FirstOfNames` live in `tests/performance/conftest.py` only to count the Python side; [history.md](history.md) lists the missing text and date probes as open. `CountMixed` is the exception that made the rule worth stating: the host cannot tell a mixed range apart either, but the rule that admits one is the whole point of the type, so it earns a probe and a control to be read against.
+The column look, a column of numbers, has no probe, because the host cannot tell that range from any other, so `FirstOfNumbers` lives in `tests/performance/conftest.py` only to count the Python side. The date and text looks are probes, `FirstOfDates` and `FirstOfNames`, for the rule each applies to what the host cannot tell apart, so the runners have rows for them. `CountMixed` is the exception that made the rule worth stating: the host cannot tell a mixed range apart either, but the rule that admits one is the whole point of the type, so it earns a probe and a control to be read against.
 
 ## What is deliberately absent, and why
 
diff --git a/docs/seam/host.md b/docs/seam/host.md
index a4b4920..4346e4a 100644
--- a/docs/seam/host.md
+++ b/docs/seam/host.md
@@ -47,7 +47,7 @@ flowchart TD
     Error -->|"nothing registered"| Name["Python functions are names Excel does not know, and GoosedoveStatus shows the error"]
 ```
 
-Every probe is hidden: `=Zero()` works, and the wizard never lists Zero. `=Add(1,2)` returns 3 and proves a number goes in and out, at the seam's cost alone. There are sixteen in `probes.py`; Ping and the library's functions, one module per group under `src/goosedove/excel/`, are the Python functions registered visibly. Because the description Excel-DNA is given is the docstring, a probe's "Probe: ..." line is what the wizard would show if it were not hidden.
+Every probe is hidden: `=Zero()` works, and the wizard never lists Zero. `=Add(1,2)` returns 3 and proves a number goes in and out, at the seam's cost alone. There are eighteen in `probes.py`; Ping and the library's functions, one module per group under `src/goosedove/excel/`, are the Python functions registered visibly. Because the description Excel-DNA is given is the docstring, a probe's "Probe: ..." line is what the wizard would show if it were not hidden.
 
 Explicit exports is a setting in the project file under which only methods marked as worksheet functions reach the sheet by themselves; everything else the sheet can call is registered on purpose. Three C# methods are marked as worksheet functions, GoosedoveStatus, GoosedoveHandles and HostAdd, and one as a command, GoosedovePrepareToQuit. The Python functions arrive through the lists above. A public C# method that is not marked is not a worksheet function: `=RegisterAll()` shows `#NAME?`, and the smoke test, which runs in a real Excel, checks that it does.
 
